  "prediction": "**Summary of the Department of Defense's Foreign Currency Budget Rates**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) establishes foreign currency budget rates to determine the value of foreign currencies in relation to the US dollar for overseas expenditures. These rates are used to calculate funding needs for Operation and Maintenance (O&M) and Military Personnel (MILPERS) accounts. The report examines DOD's methodology for establishing foreign currency budget rates and its impact on projected funding needs.\n\n**Methodology and Changes**\n\nDOD has used various methodologies to establish foreign currency budget rates, including a centered weighted average model. In 2014, DOD revised its methodology to more closely align with market rates, resulting in a decrease in projected funding needs. The report notes that DOD's revised rates have been more accurate in reflecting market fluctuations.\n\n**Impact on Funding Needs**\n\nThe report finds that DOD's revised foreign currency budget rates have resulted in a decrease in projected funding needs for O&M and MILPERS accounts. For example, in fiscal years 2014-2016, DOD's revised rates resulted in a decrease of approximately $1.3 billion in projected funding needs.\n\n**Data Issues and Recommendations**\n\nThe report identifies data issues with the Army's Foreign Currency Fluctuation reports, which hinder the ability to track foreign currency gains and losses. The report recommends that DOD:\n\n1. Review foreign currency rates used for disbursements to ensure they are consistent with market rates.\n2. Analyze the size of the Foreign Currency Fluctuation Defense (FCFD) account to ensure it is sufficient to cover potential losses.\n3. Develop a plan to modify the Army's Foreign Currency Fluctuation reports to accurately track foreign currency gains and losses.\n4. Develop a plan to adjust the FCFD account to ensure it is sufficient to cover potential losses.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe report concludes that DOD's revised foreign currency budget rates have resulted in a decrease in projected funding needs for O&M and MILPERS accounts. However, data issues with the Army's Foreign Currency Fluctuation reports hinder the ability to track foreign currency gains and losses. The report recommends that DOD take steps to address these issues and ensure that its foreign currency budget rates accurately reflect market fluctuations.",
